$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The challenge</h2>
<p>In a pending HMA petition before Family Court-02, the trial judge assigned the recording of evidence to a Local Commissioner. The petitioner moved the High Court under Article 227, contending that evidence should be recorded in the courtroom with the judge present to manage disputes as they arise — a concern grounded, according to counsel, in the nature and conduct of the respondent.</p>

<h2>Why the High Court declined to interfere</h2>
<p>Justice Shankar accepted the practical premise of commissioner-recorded evidence: a Family Court judge handling a full daily board cannot devote continuous sittings to a single trial, and insisting on open-court recording "may not be practical in nature". Decisive, though, was the quality of the Family Court's order — described as fairly elaborate and as taking into account "various eventualities". With safeguards built into the appointment itself, no supervisory correction was warranted; any live difficulty during the recording can be carried back to the Family Court.</p>

<h2>How commissioner-recorded evidence works in Delhi matrimonial trials</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Appointment.</strong> After issues are framed, the Family Court appoints a Local Commissioner — commonly a practising advocate — fixing fees, venue and a calendar of dates.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Affidavit in chief.</strong> Examination-in-chief is tendered by affidavit; the witness appears before the commissioner for cross-examination.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Recording.</strong> Depositions are recorded verbatim; objections are noted, not decided — admissibility rulings remain with the court. Demeanour and any disruption are minuted.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Report.</strong> The commissioner returns the record to the Family Court, which reads the evidence, hears arguments and delivers judgment.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Commissioner proceedings are court proceedings. Non-appearance carries the same consequences as absence before the judge, and conduct before the commissioner — recorded in the minutes — can colour the court\'s view of a party. Prepare for a commissioner date exactly as for a court date.</p>
</div>

<h2>Strategic notes</h2>
<p>For parties anxious about an aggressive opponent, the better course than resisting the appointment is shaping it: seek specific directions in the appointment order — videography where justified, security of the venue, a schedule that prevents drip-feed adjournments, and liberty to approach the court on 48 hours\' notice if recording is obstructed. For the party seeking expedition, a commissioner calendar is usually the fastest path through a heavy witness list, and Delhi Family Courts grant it readily. And for both sides, this order is a reminder that CM(M) petitions against case-management directions rarely succeed and cost months; the energy is better spent inside the trial framework the Family Court has built.</p>
